WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
We've all been hearing that deep neural networks work really well for a lot of problems.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:07.960
It's not just that they need to be big neural networks, it's that specifically they need

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.040
to be deep or to have a lot of hidden layers.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.040
So why is that?

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.680
Let's go for a couple examples and try to gain some intuition for why deep networks

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.920
might work well.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.560
So first, what is a deep network computing?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.840
If you're building a system for face recognition or face detection, here's what a deep neural

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.160
network could be doing.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.520
Perhaps you input a picture of a face, then the first layer of the neural network you can

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.480
think of as maybe being a feature detector or an edge detector.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.340
In this example, I'm plotting what a neural network with maybe 20 hidden units might be

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:51.720
trying to compute on this image with the 20 hidden units visualized by these little square

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:52.720
boxes.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.900
So for example, this little visualization represents a hidden unit that's trying to

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:02.620
figure out if you know where the edges of that orientation are in the image.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:08.900
And maybe this hidden unit might be trying to figure out where are the horizontal edges

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.460
in this image.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.940
And when we talk about convolutional networks in a later course, this particular visualization

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:15.940
will make a bit more sense.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:19.380
But informally, you can think of the first layer of the neural network as looking at

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.100
a picture and trying to figure out where are the edges in this picture.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.180
Now that it's figured out where are the edges in this picture by grouping together pixels

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:33.700
to form edges, it can then take the detected edges and group edges together to form parts

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:34.700
of faces.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.540
So for example, you might have a little neuron trying to see if it's finding an eye

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.900
or a different neuron trying to find that part of the nose.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:50.020
And so by putting together lots of edges, it can start to detect different parts of

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.420
faces.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.860
And then finally, by putting together different parts of faces, like an eye or a nose or an

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:04.380
ear or a chin, it can then try to recognize or detect different types of faces.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.860
So intuitively, you can think of the earlier layers of a neural network as detecting simpler

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:14.140
functions like edges and then composing them together in the later layers of a neural

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:18.940
network so that they can learn more and more complex functions.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:23.980
These visualizations will make more sense when we talk about convolutional nets.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.620
And one technical detail of this visualization, the edge detectors are looking in relatively

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.540
small areas of an image, maybe very small regions like that.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:37.540
And then the facial detectors, you know, can look at maybe much larger areas of the

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:38.540
image.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.860
But the main intuition, what you take away from this is just finding simpler things

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:46.060
like edges and then building them up, composing them together to detect more complex

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.540
things like an eye or a nose and then composing those together to find even more

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.940
complex things.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.940
And this type of simple to complex hierarchical representation or compositional

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:03.580
representation applies in other types of data than images and face recognition as

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:04.580
well.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.540
For example, if you're trying to build a speech recognition system, it's hard to

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.700
visualize speech, but if you input an audio clip, then maybe the first level of

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:19.220
a neural network might learn to detect, you know, low level audio waveform features,

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:24.500
such as is this tone going up, is this going down, is it the white noise or sibilant

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:29.980
sound like right, and what is the pitch, but it can detect low level waveform

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.260
features like that.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.460
And then by composing low level waveforms, maybe you'll learn to detect basic

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:36.940
units of sound.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:40.460
So in linguistics, they're called phonemes.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:45.620
But for example, in the word cat, the cup is a phoneme, the ah is a phoneme, the

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.420
tuh is another phoneme, but learns to find maybe the basic units of sound and then

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:55.220
composing that together, maybe you learn to recognize words in the audio and then

00:03:55.220 --> 00:04:03.100
maybe compose those together in order to recognize entire phrases or sentences.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:06.540
So deep neural network with multiple hidden layers might be able to have the

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.380
earlier layers learn these low level simpler features and then have the later

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:16.020
deeper layers then put together the simpler things that's detected in order

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.180
to detect more complex things like recognize specific words or even phrases

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:25.820
or sentences that you're uttering in order to carry out speech recognition.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:30.100
And what we see is that whereas the earlier layers are computing what seems

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:35.100
like relatively simple functions of the input, such as where the edges, by the

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.340
time you get deep in the network, you can actually do, you know, surprisingly

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:45.020
complex things such as detect faces or detect words or phrases or sentences.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.300
Some people like to make an analogy between deep neural networks and the

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.140
human brain where we believe or neuroscientists believe that the human

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:57.340
brain also starts off detecting simple things like edges in what your eyes see

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:01.660
and then builds those up to detect more complex things like the faces that

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.580
you see.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.580
I think analogies between deep learning and the human brain are sometimes a

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:12.620
little bit dangerous but you know there is a lot of truth to this being how we

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:16.500
think the human brain works and that the human brain probably detect simple

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.860
things like edges first and it puts them together to form more and more

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:25.140
complex objects and so that has served as a loose form of inspiration

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.820
for some deep learning as well.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:31.700
We'll say a bit more about the human brain or about the biological brain in

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:35.480
a later video this week.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:41.360
The other piece of intuition about why deep networks seem to work well is

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.060
the following.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:47.880
So this result comes from circuit theory which pertains to thinking

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.200
about what types of functions you can compute with different AND gates

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.280
and OR gates and NOT gates, basically logic gates.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:58.120
So informally their functions compute with a relatively small but deep

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:02.760
neural network and by small I mean the number of hidden units is relatively

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:06.520
small but that if you try to compute the same function with a shallow

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.200
network so beyond the loud enough hidden layers then you might require

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.480
exponentially more hidden units to compute.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:18.440
So let me just give you one example and illustrate this a bit informally.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:21.960
But let's say you're trying to compute the exclusive OR or the parity

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:23.160
of all your input features.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:31.040
So you're trying to compute X1, X4, X2, X4, X3, X4 up to Xn if you have

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.280
n or nx features.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:41.200
So if you build an XOR tree like this, so first compute the XOR of X1 and X2,

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.840
then take X3 and X4 and compute their XOR and technically if you're just

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.520
using AND, OR and NOT gates you might need a couple layers to compute the

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:55.520
XOR function rather than just one layer but with a relatively small circuit

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.480
you can compute the XOR and so on.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:06.400
And then you can build really an XOR tree like so.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:12.360
And so eventually you have a circuit here that outputs the, let's call this Y,

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:17.480
that outputs Y hat equals Y, the exclusive OR or the parity of all of these

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:18.720
input bits.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.320
So to compute the XOR the depth of the network will be on the order of

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:27.760
log n, right, with this type of XOR tree.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:31.440
So the number of nodes and the number of circuit, circuit components or the

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.080
number of gates in this network is not that large.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:38.760
You don't need that many gates in order to compute the exclusive OR.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.760
But now if you're not allowed to use a new network with multiple hidden

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.160
layers with in this case all the log n hidden layers, if you're forced to

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:53.160
compute this function with just one hidden layer, right, so you have all

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:59.120
these things going into, you know, set of hidden units and then these things

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:05.840
then outputs Y. Then in order to compute the parity of XOR, to compute this

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:11.480
XOR function, this hidden layer will need to be exponentially large.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:17.120
Because essentially you need to exhaustively enumerate all 2 to the n

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:23.560
possible configurations or on the order of 2 to the n possible configurations of

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:28.000
the input bits that result in the exclusive OR being either 1 or 0.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.560
So you end up needing a hidden layer that is exponentially large in the number of

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.640
bits. I think technically you could do this with 2 to the n minus 1 hidden

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:40.560
units, right, but that's the order 2 to the n.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:44.040
So it's going to be exponentially large in the number of bits.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:49.960
So I hope this gives a sense that there are mathematical functions that are much

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:55.440
easier to compute with deep networks than with shallow networks.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.520
I have to admit, I personally found the result from circuit theory less useful

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:04.960
for gaining intuitions, but this is one of the results that people often cite

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:11.360
when just, when explaining the value of having very deep representations.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.840
Now, in addition to these reasons for preferring deep neural networks, to be

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:21.280
perfectly honest, I think the other reason the term deep learning has taken off is

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:24.440
just branding, right? These things used to be called neural networks with a lot of

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:28.840
hidden layers, but the phrase deep learning, you know, it's just a great

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:34.080
brand. It's just, it's so deep, right? So I think that once that term called on,

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.240
that really neural networks rebranded or neural networks with many hidden layers

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:44.000
rebranded help to capture the popular imagination as well. But regardless of

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:49.240
the PR branding, deep networks do work well. Sometimes people go overboard and

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.720
insist on using tons of hidden layers, but when I'm starting out on a new

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:56.800
problem, I'll often really start out with even logistic regression, then try

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:02.080
something with one or two hidden layers and use that as a hyper parameter, use

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:06.200
that as a parameter or hyper parameter that you tune in order to try to find

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:09.560
the right depth for your neural network. But over the last several years, there

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:14.560
has been a trend toward people finding that for some applications, very, very

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:18.840
deep neural networks, you know, maybe many dozens of layers sometimes can

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:23.640
sometimes be the best model for a problem. So that's it for the

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:28.880
intuitions for why deep learning seems to work well. Let's now take a look at

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:32.880
the mechanics of how to implement not just forward propagation, but also back

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:33.720
propagation.
